          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": ["The Lenstra-Lenstra-Lovász (LLL) algorithm reduces a lattice basis in polynomial time by iteratively enforcing two conditions: size reduction (each basis vector's projections onto previous vectors are small) and the Lovász condition (successive Gram-Schmidt vectors don't decrease too rapidly). The key insight is that each swap operation strictly increases a potential function, bounding the total number of swaps at O(n² log B) where B bounds the input."]
          },
          "metadata": {"model_slug": "gpt-4o"}
        }
      },
